{"author":{"id":"673e1e1b6fccff4da5524407c8a8cdc7caf18221aabb93f2bb36ffef5c2a95e8"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1781550361,"nonce":"m2YqAUipCueAA0iBNf4WADvwatE=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lAHOSn4-Gc8AAAABFXFHm87E_gIo"},"target":"809bba09ec89c3cc71ef3c6fbd449123ed33b5909a977c46e0001dd1370e2ba7","message":"\u003e _Edited 2026-06-15: corrected the premise — `pickInstanceType` does **not** rank ServerClasses by price (Karpenter core does the cheapest-first selection). The \"Refactor needed\" section has been re-scoped accordingly._\n\nFollow-up to #15. Karpenter's scheduler selects the instance type — cheapest compatible offering first, from the priced list this provider serves via `GetInstanceTypes` → `instancetype.Provider.List` — and **pins it** in the NodeClaim's `node.kubernetes.io/instance-type` requirement. The provider's `pickInstanceType` (`pkg/cloudprovider/cloudprovider.go:227`) just reads that pinned type and looks it up; `instance.Create` then creates **one** pool with `Desired: 1` (`pkg/providers/instance/instance.go:133,150`).\n\nIf that pool is never **fulfilled** — the spot bid loses the auction, or the ServerClass is capacity-constrained — the NodeClaim is stuck until Karpenter's registration TTL recreates it (and re-scheduling will usually re-pick the same cheapest class, so it can wedge repeatedly).\n\n### Why this can't be a synchronous Create-time fallback (unlike GCP)\n- **AWS** hands EC2 Fleet up to 60 candidates and lets the fleet allocation strategy pick at launch.\n- **GCP** price-sorts the compatible types and tries them cheapest-first in a loop, falling through on a *synchronous* `tryCreateInstance` error.\n- **Rackspace**: the pool API takes a **single** ServerClass, and `instance.Create` returns as soon as the pool is created via API. Fulfillment (winning the bid / a node registering) is **asynchronous**. So there is no synchronous error at Create time to fall through on — a GCP-style loop can't observe the failure.\n\nTherefore the fallback must live in a **reconciliation loop** that observes fulfillment state over time.\n\n### Sketch\n- **New reconciler** (or extend an existing controller) keyed on Karpenter-managed pools.\n- **Signal for \"not fulfilled\":** inspect the pool's `WonCount` / `Status` — already surfaced on the `Pool` struct (`pkg/providers/instance/instance.go:57,61`) and populated from the SDK in `spotToPool`/`onDemandToPool` — and/or no Node registered for the NodeClaim within a grace period.\n- **Action:** roll the pool to the **next-cheapest** compatible ServerClass and update the NodeClaim's `node.kubernetes.io/instance-type` label so state stays consistent.\n- **Bounding:** cap attempts (don't cycle forever); log the attempt order and the reason each class was abandoned (no silent truncation).\n\n### What this actually requires (the original \"refactor\" framing was wrong)\nThe provider does **not** rank ServerClasses by price today. `pickInstanceType` only reads the type Karpenter already pinned (unchanged since the initial CloudProvider impl); the cheapest-first selection happens in **Karpenter core's scheduler**, fed the priced offering list via `GetInstanceTypes` → `instancetype.Provider.List`. So there is no provider-side price-ranking helper to \"factor out.\"\n\nThe fallback therefore has to:\n- Build its **own** price-ranked list of compatible ServerClasses for the capacity type (reuse `instancetype.Provider.List` plus the same offering/price data the scheduler sees), and\n- **Override Karpenter's pinned instance type** when rolling the pool — not merely iterate a local list — keeping the NodeClaim's `instance-type` label and the pool's ServerClass in sync.\n\nA small shared \"rank compatible instance types by price\" helper is still worth adding so the reconciler's ordering matches the scheduler's intent, but it is **new** logic, not an extraction from `pickInstanceType`.\n\n### Open questions\n- Grace period before declaring a pool unfulfilled (spot auctions settle on their own cadence — confirm with Rackspace).\n- Interaction with Karpenter's own NodeClaim registration TTL / disruption — avoid double-handling (provider rolls the pool vs. Karpenter recreates the NodeClaim).\n- Does rolling the ServerClass in place play nicely with the `nodelink` providerID rewrite, or should it recreate the pool under the same NodeClaim UID?\n- Relationship to #17 (locked bid never re-evaluated): both are \"pool stuck after Create\" problems naturally owned by a reconciler. Decide whether one reconciler owns both fulfillment-fallback and re-bid/drift, or they stay separate.\n\n### Acceptance\nA pool unfulfilled past the grace period is replaced by the next-cheapest compatible ServerClass, bounded and logged, without leaking the prior pool.","files":null},{"type":5,"timestamp":1781334973,"nonce":"e9RPTU7x9vqOKzp8L8EbwGpRZNo=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADOSn4-Gc8AAAABFXFHm88AAAAGN7yQRg"},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"timestamp":1781551083,"nonce":"ZoSgcsP4kf0DuPfr9YJa4W6UF+g=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOSn4-Gc8AAAABGNQI0g","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/karpenter-provider-rackspace-spot/issues/22#issuecomment-4711516370"},"message":"\u003e This behavior changed in v2.4 — the migration guide mentions it but it's easy to miss. Adding a note to the docs PR.\n\nCould you elaborate on this?","files":null}]}